edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f113768c-cf48-5fce-945b-53b72f33cbc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527817 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527817 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527817 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527817 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527817 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527817 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527817 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527817 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186527777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527817 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2c3bc565-6668-5708-9572-740391604ada" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527867 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527867 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527867 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527867 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527867 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527867 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527867 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527867 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186527489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527867 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8b9c47c5-4e96-58fa-ac91-5791dc11f30c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527895 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527895 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527895 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527895 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527895 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527895 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527895 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527895 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186527469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527895 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d86ee8d9-aeae-5ab1-b4a0-98c765570dca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527925 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527925 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527925 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527925 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f5/f5f5b235b54d2acd20297ab365d9b93907111b3fec9a86776c28e93fdfe105ee.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(2,3),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2t,\\,3t⟩.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527925 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f9d1bf2-2ca6-5610-aed1-5f5054aae288" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527925 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(2,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(0+t(2-0)),\\,(0+t(3-0))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2t)^{2}+(3t)^{2} \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}+9{t}^{2} \\\\  & =13{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}13{t}^{2}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =39{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527980 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527980 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124172" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527980 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527980 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/09/09a38bb4306728f97b7fecbf9ab1bda7aa957607352c1ab27af09489c271c3ed.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}({y}^{2}-2x)\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(2,3),$ as shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527980 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ada3d128-ac86-51c2-88c8-3e672e4cad88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527980 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(0,1)$ and $(2,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(0+t(2-0)),\\,(1+t(3-1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,1+2t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-2x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(1+2t)^{2}-2(2t) \\\\  & =1+4t+4{t}^{2}-4t \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{4}{3}+1)-2(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527992 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527992 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527992 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527992 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/88/88da0f1ddf1f605186c9d921a9eade24b5f0aa9de2ae4afdc51a921586093b16.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-2,3)$ to the point $(0,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2t-2,\\,3-3t⟩.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527992 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca5fbd9d-06c2-5516-a2aa-caaa1b2a1fc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527992 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-2,3)$ and $(0,0).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(-2+t(0-(-2))),\\,(3+t(0-3))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,\\,3-3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-2)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2t-2)^{2}(3-3t) \\\\  & =4(t-1)^{2}\\cdot 3(1-t) \\\\  & =-12(t-1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-12(t-1)^{3}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-24{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
